From: Eric Watt Forste <arkuat@netcom.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Mar 1994 19:37:24 -0800 (PST)
Subject: [#94-3-277] PCR: replies to Starr, Phoenix, Jones, et al.

In message #94-3-223 on Thursday, March 17, Tim Starr wrote:

> There are positions known as "realist" in both metaphysics and episte-
> mology.  In epistemology, realism is the view that knowing subjects
> are directly aware of the objects of knowledge, which have independent
> existence and identity apart from the knower.  This is in distinction
> from both representationalism, a la Descartes, Locke, Hume, and most
> Modern philosophers, which is the view that knowing subjects are
> directly aware of representations of the objects of knowledge, and
> from idealism, a la Kant, Hegel, etc., which is the view that the
> objects of knowledge are constituted by knowing subjects; if not
> actually brought into existence by them, then given identity by their
> faculties.

Some studies which I regret I cannot cite from memory have shown that
vision in frogs returns exactly four nerve impulses to the frogs
brain. I believe they are sustained contrast, moving edge, sudden
darkening, and nearby small convex objects. That's it. That's all the
useful information a frog gets from its eyes. It's enough for the
frog, or rather, it's enough for the frog's genes.

Human eyes and neurovisual systems are much more complex and
functional than those of frogs, yet are also highly selective and
fallible, screening out all kinds of potentially useful (to us)
information which natural selection has shown to be not terribly
useful as far as the propagation of our genes is concerned.

I have no idea what "direct awareness" has to do with any of this,
except that it sounds like mysticism to me. Our senses are fallible
specific systems developed by natural selection for specific purposes.
Perfect knowledge of all elements of our surroundings, or "direct
awareness" of anything, are computationally expensive and
evolutionarily maladaptive. Even if "direct awareness" is possible, my
review of Hume's INQUIRY CONCERNING HUMAN UNDERSTANDING shows that his
argument against any rational justification for the induction of
causality depends in no way on this particular "realist" vs.
representationalist dichotomy. His arguments apply just as thoroughly
to epistemological realists as they do to representationalists. Unless
you claim that we are somehow "directly aware" of causation, that we
can actually *observe* causation, and not mere correlation. That would
clearly be plain mysticism, but I don't think you would go that far.

> >Now, clearly, we can have different degrees of confidence in the truth
> >of a matter...
> 
> I'm not sure what this can mean - that we intend to act as if some
> things were really really true, and others not very true at all?
> It seems more accurate to say that it will take more criticism to
> get us to reject some positions than others which are less strongly
> held.

If I have a very high degree of confidence about the truth of a
proposition, I will hedge the bets I make based on the truth of that
proposition less than I would hedge the bets I make based on the truth
of propositions I am less certain about. But your point about relative
degrees of criticism corresponds to ones I've made earlier. If we find
that two beliefs of which we are certain lead to a logical
contradiction, we reject the belief of which we are less certain.
 
> Isn't the notion that "ultimately we have to come to some fundamental
> axiomatic idea(s) which we can only defend by appeals to faith or
> intuition" itself a fundamental axiomatic idea which can only be
> defended by faith or intuition?  If not, isn't it founded upon such
> a thing?

No, it is not. But you are welcome to refute it if you can. I make
that statement groundlessly; I do not justify it in any way. But I
will continue to believe it until you show me otherwise, by justifying
all of your strongly-held beliefs by valid deduction from axioms which
are "self-justifying" or whatever. I might even abandon it if you
simply show me how it logically contradicts some other more closely
held belief of mine, or if you can show me how it would be more useful
for me to reject it than to continue to hold it. The possible
criticisms are many, and I leave it to you to choose the most
effective one. But the criticism that my statement is groundless or
ultimately unprovable will not impress me.

This is how PCR works. Blind variation and selective retention. My
statement is an example of blind variation. If you can refute my
statement, I will demonstrate selective retention by weeding that
statement out of my personal meme pool.

#

In message #94-3-233 on Saturday, March 19, Stephen J. Whitrow wrote:

> In PCR there would be many very high credibility beliefs; certainties
> would have to be fairly tautological and impossible to falsify, e.g.
> "no lights are black".  With the possibility of uplifted beasts, I
> doubt if you could have "no horses are men" in PCR.  I think "there is
> a material world" would be ok.

Actually, PCRists who have thought hard about the matter see "there is
a material world" as contingent on Ockham's Razor and well-established
scientific theory, namely Darwin's. We know that a material world
exists because of what we know about how our minds came into existence
in the first place. We know that metaphysical idealism is false
because Darwin has explained the existence of minds by material
processes that happened before there were minds to perceive them,
because Darwin's explanation has withstood all criticism (and there
has been a lot of it) to date better than any alternative
explanations, and because Ockham shaves Bishop Berkeley's God off the
face of the universe.    (This last appealing image and the line of
argument in this paragraph are both due to Bartley.)

#
 
In message #94-3-236 on Saturday, March 19, Reilly Jones wrote:

> Your subjective states of mind are subjective because only you have
> access to them.  To other people, they are ojective reality which they
> are barred from knowing by an epistemological boundary.  This boundary
> does not exist within your own mind.  The distinction between
> subjective reality and objective reality is not spurious, it is
> critical to identify in epistemology.  Indeed, it is what a large part
> of philosophical and neuroscientific thought has been all about.

Epistemological boundary? What epistemological boundary is that?

I would say that your subjective states of mind are objective reality
which other people are barred from knowing by technological limits,
just as technological limits used to bar people from the observation
of erythrocytes, distant galaxies, and electrons. But I would not
elevate barriers of inadequate technology to the level of an
"epistemological boundary". I still contend that the distinction
between subjective reality and objective reality is wholly spurious;
subjective reality is a subset of objective reality. It's the part of
objective reality that is taking place in your nervous system.

In MATTER AND CONSCIOUSNESS, Paul Churchland discusses the fallibility
of our access to our internal mental states. "Subjective" reality is
observed fallibly and imperfectly, just the same way that objective
reality is observed. Experimental evidence for this is well
established, and Churchland provides some good references to the
literature. My observation of and reporting on my present "subjective"
mental state is neither more nor less fallible than my observation of
and reporting on the objects on my desk at this moment.

> Utility points to purpose.  Because individuals are capable of having
> several purposes at the same time, which can be incompatible with each
> other, contradictory knowledge can be possessed by the individual
> because the foundation of knowledge is purpose.  PCR, using
> evolutionary epistemology, uses fitness as the criteria for the
> selection of ideas that are "known."  Fitness always refers to
> purpose.  Biological evolutionary fitness refers to the purpose of
> survival.  Epistemological evolutionary fitness refers to whatever
> purposes an individual has chosen, which may include survival.  Thus,
> PCR's connection with evolutionary epistemology is simply the modern
> equivalent of Pyrrho's dictum from 320 BC that "Reason is merely the
> sophist servant of desire."

Fitness is purpose is utility, eh? Now I begin to understand how
E-prime might be useful in discussions such as these. I was noticing
when I was reviewing some of Rand's epistemology the other day how
objectivists seem to be fond of beginning arguments by asserting. "A
is B. B is C. And so therefore..."

But to the point.

Because utility is slippery and ill-defined, because there is no
standard of measurement for the "utile" that we can use to measure
your utility against mine, etc, utility is not a particularly useful
criticism in and of itself. It includes too much. It is too general.
It is too ill-defined. It is only useful for distinguishing between
ideas which have *already* been thoroughly analyzed for logical
consistency, and have *already* been experimentally tested against
sense data, or that do not admit of experimental test against sense
data. It is *not* a criticism of first resort. Attacking PCR on the
basis that judging ideas based on their utility (without paying any
attention to their validity or accordance to sense data) lets you
believe whatever you want to believe is attacking a straw man. Sense
data will not allow a pancritical rationalist to believe whatever she
wants; logical consistency will not allow a pancritical rationalist to
believe whatever is convenient to her ends. Pyrrho is not PCR.

> No, ultimately the evolution of knowledge is founded upon the basis of
> memetic evolution, an entirely new and more complex selectional system
> than biological evolution.

Sure, but are you denying that, for the time being, memetic evolution
is grounded in a biological substrate? When a baby touches a candle
and gets burned, it becomes reluctant to touch candles. This is
evidence of the presence of some memes about induction and causality
in the baby's mind. Did the baby attend a lecture about induction and
causality at the birthing room? No. Our "initial-state" memes are
phenotypical products of our genes. And if you think the baby got the
ideas of induction and causality from unassisted sense-experience, you
might be interested in a book by one Hume.

