I was saying that usefulness protects noncontradiction, or more
properly, that it defeats the alternatives to noncontradiction. This
is not inconsistent because I'm not smart enough to design an
empirical experiment to refute a proposed alternative to
noncontradiction (I must admit, I haven't really given the matter much
thought). Appealing to logical validity, in this case, would of course
be begging the question. If you can explain to me how one of the
"greater" criticisms can differentiate between noncontradiction and
one of its alternatives, I would be perfectly happy to let my point
about utility drop.

> Are you saying that any time someone acts in a logically inconsistent
> manner, they are doing something stupid, wrong, or contrary to their
> interests?  This is precisely what I'm disagreeing with.

Okay. I overstated my case. Let's just say "wrong", and forget about
the "contrary to interests" part. But brainwashing is surely a
pathological case. Arguments acceded to through brainwashing cannot be
said to be rationally acceded to unless the person doing the acceding
has given informed consent to his own brainwashing. I think your point
reduces to the idea that if you vigorously mess around with the
substrate levels of biology and psychology that the levels of
epistemology are built upon, then you can produce an effect on
epistemological phenomena.

> I agree that people generally don't go around thinking A <=> ~A.  But
> for many people, telling them that A <=> B, B <=> C, C <=> D, D <=> E,
> and E <=> ~A will get nowhere--except that they will resent you for
> being "smart" or even "stuck-up."  Most people won't sit still for a
