> taught to distrust logic, so that even though they would reject an
> obvious contradiction, they would also reject any input that looked
> like a proof, before they ever had a chance to apply it to their
> beliefs.  You may belittle such people...

But you cannot reason with them. PCR doesn't have any answers for
people who are just flat out not interested in reasoning things out.
The only sort of irrationalist it defeats is the existentialist type
who insists that *reason* is what has led him to be an irrationalist
because reason has shown him that no beliefs can be justified, so
therefore he can believe whatever he feels like, and that the
rationalists "faith" in reason is an equally irrational commitment.
These arguments (and they are arguments, carefully reasoned but
incorrect) for nihilism are what Bartley aimed at and defeated. So
one cannot make a rational argument for irrationalism. 

But an irrational "argument" for irrationalism? Well, sure. There's no
magic refutation for that. If the thug with the gun doesn't want to
discuss the matter, then you'd better make sure you draw first. Or for
a more everyday example, if your churchgoing relative gets to the
point where they claim they aren't interested in any more of that
high-falutin' logic, well, sure... there are no more rational
arguments you can appeal to at that point.

> ...but there are a lot of them out there.  The various celibate-clergy
> rules haven't selected against religion.  And any opponent is worth
> worrying about if they have superior force or numbers.

But the "worrying about" that sort of opponent you do is worrying
about how you will cryptographically and militarily defend yourself
