>   Putting dangerous technology into the hands of many, may be safer
> than isolating it in the hands of a few.

Yes.  The question of "do I wish to accept this technology", therefore,
becomes more compelling when the technology is inherently isolated in
the hands of a few.  Mass media is such a technology -- TV and radio
stations, national magazines, etc. -- the more massive, the more
power is wielded by a small number.  This is the type of technology
I am thinking of when I caution against techno-fetishism.

I don't watch television because the linear image-feed leaves me no
time to critique the message.  If I'm reading a newspaper the bias may
be the same but at least I get a chance to think about it.

When reading this list I get information that comes from or through
one other person, who has less power over me than a large organization.
Therefore, even if the information is equally bad, it has less chance
of being dangerous to my freedom of action.

