From: timstarr@netcom.com (Tim Starr)
Date: Thu, 24 Mar 1994 21:35:39 -0800
Subject: [#94-3-281] Grigsby's Fallacy

>From: grigsby@agames.com (JUMP IN THE FIRE)
>
>...I remember a statement to the effect of "Automobiles don't 
>pollute enough to infringe on your right to breathable air".

That was probably mine, and what I maintain is that there is no proof that
car pollution is a significant threat to public health.

>  A study
>I read about showed that kids growing up in the LA basin have demonstrable
>permanent lung damage resulting in reduced lung capacity for life.

If this study is correct, it still does nothing to prove that this is
caused by car pollution.  It indicates that growing up in the LA basin is
a health hazard, not that this is caused by car pollution.  There are
non-anthropogenic factors which may easily be the cause: the basin
itself, which traps suspended particulates that might be more widely
diffused otherwise, and the desert climate, which might lead to the
presence of more suspended particulates (dust) than in more hospitable
climes.

Dislike of the smell of car exhaust is not a public health hazard.  If
we're going to legislate against aesthetic torts, then I'd like to file
a class-action suit against Madonna.

Someone as concerned with accusing his critics of fallacies as Grigsby
ought to be better at avoiding such non-sequiturs.

